# Caddy in front of Egg. It gets and renews a free HTTPS certificate on its own.
#
# Ports (open them in your firewall and on your cloud provider):
#   80/tcp   needed: the certificate check and the redirect to https
#   443/tcp  needed: the site
#   443/udp  optional: HTTP/3
#
# APP_HOST is the host name part of APP_URL, for example egg.example.com (no https://, no path).
# Its DNS record must already point at this server, or Caddy cannot get a certificate.
# For a trial on one machine, APP_HOST=localhost makes Caddy use its own local certificate.
#
# Caddy replaces any X-Forwarded-For sent by the client with the real address, which is what
# TRUST_PROXY=1 relies on. The app is never published on a port of its own: only Caddy is.
#
# The GitHub webhook is public and unauthenticated until its signature is checked, so Caddy
# refuses a body over 5 MiB before the app sees it. The app holds the same cap (MAX_BODY_BYTES in
# web/src/app/api/webhooks/github/route.ts) and also limits concurrent webhook reads, so this line
# is a backstop, not the only check. Keep the two values equal.
#
# The stock caddy:2 image cannot rate-limit requests or cap concurrent connections per path:
# both need a third-party module, which this file does not add. For those limits, use your
# firewall or cloud provider's request limits on /api/webhooks/github (see docs/selfhost).
{$APP_HOST} {
	encode zstd gzip

	@github path /api/webhooks/github
	request_body @github {
		max_size 5MiB
	}

	reverse_proxy app:3000
}
